  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nRationale:\n- This problem involves minimizing the maximum overlap of a step function h(x) and its shifted versions (1-h(x+k))\n- We start from the parent solution with 45 samples (given as initial_h_values) which achieves C\u22480.3808\n- The key insight is to refine this solution by exploiting its high autocorrelation properties, focusing optimization on regions with large overlaps\n- We need to enforce constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)\u224845/2 (since \u222bh(x)dx=1 with dx=2/45)\n- The challenge is to balance global exploration and local refinement to find a better C bound\n\nApproach:\n- We use a trust-region reflective optimization to enforce bounds (h[i]\u2208[0,1])\n- We reformulate the equality constraint (\u222bh(x)dx=1) by parameterizing h to satisfy exactly\n- The objective is max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx computed via cross-correlation\n- We implement a sequence of mutations: global perturbations, local adjustments, and polished to achieve bolder improvements while respecting the interval constraints and ensuring smooth progress\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables and objective:\n- x \u2208 \u211d\u2074\u2075 satisfies 0\u2264x[i]\u22641 and \u2211x[i]=45/2 exactly\n- Objective function: C = \u211ddx * max_{k=-44 to 44) \u2211_i x[i] * (1-x[i+k]) (for correlated indices)\n- Constraints:\n  - Box constraints: x[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n  - Linear constraint: \u2211x[i] = 45/2\n\nRepresentation:\n- Represent h(x) by its 45 coefficients, a 45-dimensional vector\n- Use vectorization in objective computations via cross-correlation\n- Ensure uniqueness by enforcing x[i]>0 for some i and proper interval occupation\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialization:\n- Validate initial_h_values: ensure (np.max(initial_h_values)<=1, np.min(initial_h_values)>=0, and sum(initial_h_values)==45/2)\n- If not, either project or fail\n\nMutations:\n1. Global mutation:\n   - Add uniform perturbation: x[i] \u2192 min(1, max(0, x[i] + uniform(-0.3,0.3)))\n   - Then project to satisfy \u2211x[i]=45/2\n   - Track progress by comparing C score\n\n2. Local mutation:\n   - Select indices where >0 and <1\n   - For each position i, find neighbors i-k where cross-correlation is large\n   - Update x[i] based on local behavior: move towards min/max of h[i+k] based on sign of local gradient\n   - Maintain smoothness: ensure changes are proportional to original values\n\nTermination conditions:\n- When no improvement (pre-local change)\n- Or time budget exceeded\n- Or solution stabilized\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nMutation scheme:\n- Phase1: Global perturbation (achieve better overall bound)\n- Phase2: Local reduction (fine-tune)\n- Controlled by convergence metrics: consecutive no-improvement counts\n\nConvergence metrics:\n1. No-improvement1: function improves on mutation\n2. No-improvement2: direction log adheres to trend\n3. Max frequency: ensures not getting stuck\n\nMonitoring stops if:\n- C doesn't improve\n- Or max stagnation\n- Or time exhausted\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimate:\n- Phase1: ~42% budget on global optimizations\n- Phase2: ~58% budget on local refinements\n\nRobustness:\n- Always check constraints\n- Back up to last best solution\n- Implement incremental updates with explicit checks\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n1. Post-variation: clip values to [0,1]\n2. Re-normalize to 21.6665 by adjusting \u03b4 def\u222b(x) = sum(x)-21.6665 adjusted by interpolation\n3. Redefine goal as minimizing max_k\u2110(h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) for comparisons\n\nValidation methods:\n- Init safety checks\n- Post-optimization score re-computation\n- Consistency with parent representation\n- Maintain logging of transformation",
